## Chapter 4 (Cyclic Groups)

**Proved in the exercises files:** §4.1 Ex 23(a) (|a| = |a⁻¹|, via the new
`hasOrder` relation in `std/group-order.bpa`); §4.2 Ex 19 (a),(b),(f) (the modulus
identities |z̄|=|z|, zz̄=|z|², |zw|=|z||w|, via `std/complex-modulus.bpa`).
**Forwarded to the §4.1 main text:** Ex 32 (a coprime power generates), Ex 35
(subgroups of ℤ are nℤ), Ex 36 (generators of ℤ_n), Ex 38 (element order divides
group order).

### order-of-element as a first-class object over the whole group
The `hasOrder(g, n)` RELATION (single-valued, `orderIsUnique`) now lets orders be
stated/compared without a choice operator; what these still need is named per item.
- §4.1 Ex 23 (b),(c) — |a| = |g⁻¹ag| and |ab| = |ba|: need the
  conjugation-preserves-order lemma (g⁻¹ag)ᵏ = g⁻¹aᵏg.
- §4.1 Ex 30 — gcd(|a|,|b|)=1 ⇒ ⟨a⟩∩⟨b⟩={e}: the order-divides argument is in
  reach; needs the intersection-membership plumbing tying it to order.
- §4.1 Ex 31 — the torsion subgroup: needs a finite-order predicate + the
  order-of-product bound (gh)^{mn}=e.
- §4.1 Ex 11 — possible orders of a with a²⁴=e: needs the divisors of 24 as a set.

### finite cardinality / counting / totient φ
Now that ℤ_n / U(n) ALGEBRA is in `std/integer-mod-n.bpa`, the remaining ℤ_n
exercises are the COUNTING ones.
- §4.1 Ex 24, 25 — number of generators of ℤ_pq, ℤ_{p^r}: need Euler's totient φ.
- §4.1 Ex 29 — ℤ_n has an even number of generators for n>2: the generator set as
  a finite countable object (pair r with n−r).
- §4.1 Ex 26 — ℤ_p has no nontrivial subgroups: a prime instance of
  `integer-mod-n` + subgroup-order-divides-group.
- §4.1 Ex 1(a,b), 2, 3, 5, 9, 13 — concrete generator/order/subgroup enumeration in
  specific ℤ_n / U(n): skipped as brute, or need finite enumeration.

### trigonometry (sin / cos / π) — the §4.2 analytic gap
- §4.2 Ex 16, 17 — rectangular↔polar conversions (cis, arctan).
- §4.2 Ex 18 — powers of complex numbers via DeMoivre.
- §4.2 Ex 20, 21 — list/graph the 5th/6th roots of unity, primitive roots.
- §4.2 Ex 19 (c),(d),(e) — z⁻¹=z̄/|z|² (field inverse tie-in), and the triangle /
  reverse-triangle inequalities (Cauchy–Schwarz on real parts).

### direct products + order-of-product law
- §4.1 Ex 33 — an abelian group with two order-2 cyclic subgroups has an order-4
